<script lang="ts">
	import { Scrolly, Step } from '$lib/scrolly';
	import { MapStage } from '$lib/stages';
	import { GeoJSONSource, CircleLayer, SymbolLayer } from 'svelte-maplibre-gl';
	import { streetsStyle } from '$lib/styles';
	import BarStage from './BarStage.svelte';
	import { places } from './places';

	// Runes work inside Stories.
	const parks = $derived(places.features.filter((f) => f.properties.kind === 'park').length);

	const visits = [
		{ label: 'Parks', value: 42 },
		{ label: 'Malls', value: 65 },
		{ label: 'Museums', value: 18 },
		{ label: 'Beaches', value: 27 }
	];
</script>

This Story is a **Draft** and always will be: it is the reference for writing Stories. It lives in `src/stories/_example/index.svx`. The text is Markdown; the Scrollys are Svelte components.

## An Overlay Scrolly

In an Overlay, the Steps scroll as cards over a full-screen Stage. Each Step declares a *View*; the Stage decides how to get there.

<Scrolly layout="overlay" label="Map of Singapore">
{#snippet stage(view, progress)}
<MapStage {view} {progress} style={streetsStyle}>
<GeoJSONSource id="places" data={places}>
<CircleLayer
	id="places-circles"
	paint={{
		'circle-radius': 8,
		'circle-color': ['match', ['get', 'kind'], 'park', '#206095', '#ff6b35'],
		'circle-stroke-width': 2,
		'circle-stroke-color': '#ffffff'
	}}
/>
<SymbolLayer
	id="places-labels"
	layout={{
		'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
		'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold'],
		'text-size': 13,
		'text-offset': [0, 1.2],
		'text-anchor': 'top'
	}}
	paint={{ 'text-halo-color': '#ffffff', 'text-halo-width': 2 }}
/>
</GeoJSONSource>
</MapStage>
{/snippet}

<Step view={{ camera: { center: [103.82, 1.35], zoom: 10.5 }, layers: [] }}>

Singapore, with none of the Story's layers on.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: { center: [103.84, 1.31], zoom: 11.5 }, layers: ['places-circles', 'places-labels'] }}>

The **places** layer turns on, and the camera moves in.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: { center: [103.84, 1.31], zoom: 11.5 }, layers: ['places-circles', 'places-labels'], filters: { 'places-circles': ['==', ['get', 'kind'], 'park'], 'places-labels': ['==', ['get', 'kind'], 'park'] } }}>

A filter keeps only the {parks} parks.

</Step>

<Step scrub view={{ camera: { center: [103.8545, 1.2834], zoom: 13, pitch: 0, bearing: 0 }, to: { center: [103.8605, 1.2816], zoom: 16, pitch: 60, bearing: -40 }, layers: ['places-labels'] }}>

**Scrub:** this Step follows your scroll. Keep scrolling and the camera tilts and turns over Marina Bay.

</Step>
</Scrolly>

## A Sidecar Scrolly

In a Sidecar, the Steps sit beside the Stage. On a narrow screen it falls back to an Overlay. Any component that takes `view` and `progress` can be a Stage — this one is a bar chart from the Story's own folder.

<Scrolly layout="sidecar" label="Bar chart of visits">
{#snippet stage(view, progress)}
<BarStage {view} {progress} data={visits} />
{/snippet}

<Step view={{ grow: true }} scrub>

**Scrub** again: the bars grow as you scroll.

</Step>

<Step view={{ highlight: 'Malls' }}>

Malls get the most visits.

</Step>

<Step view={{ highlight: 'Museums' }}>

Museums get the fewest.

</Step>
</Scrolly>

## After the Scrollys

Ordinary text continues here. Lists and links get the Story typography:

1. Write the text in Markdown.
2. Declare a View on each Step.
3. Let the Stage work out how to get there.

See the [roadmap](https://github.com/bearylogical/stories/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md) for what comes next.
